## 5. System Architecture

CampusBite follows a three-layer architecture consisting of the frontend, backend and database.

### 5.1 Frontend Layer

The frontend is developed using React.js and CSS. It provides the interface through which customers, restaurant owners and delivery partners interact with the application.

The frontend includes registration and login pages, dashboards, restaurant listings, menu management, order management and profile-related interfaces.

Axios is used to send HTTP requests to the backend APIs and retrieve application data.

### 5.2 Backend Layer

The backend is developed using Python and FastAPI.

It handles application logic, API requests, user authentication, order operations, restaurant management and communication with the database.

Pydantic is used for data validation, while SQLAlchemy is used to perform database operations.

JWT authentication is used to support authenticated access to protected application features.

### 5.3 Database Layer

Microsoft SQL Server is used as the relational database.

It stores information related to users, restaurants, menu items, orders, payments, reviews and other application activities.

The backend communicates with SQL Server through SQLAlchemy.

## 6. Functional Modules

CampusBite consists of three main user modules.

### 6.1 Customer Module

The customer module allows users to access restaurant and food ordering services.

Key functionalities include:

- User registration and login.
- Browsing available restaurants.
- Searching and filtering restaurants.
- Viewing restaurant menus.
- Adding food items and placing orders.
- Viewing order history and tracking order status.
- Cancelling eligible orders.
- Submitting restaurant reviews.
- Raising return requests.
- Managing personal profile information.

### 6.2 Restaurant Owner Module

The restaurant owner module allows restaurant owners to manage their restaurant operations.

Key functionalities include:

- Managing restaurant information.
- Creating and managing menu items.
- Editing food item details and prices.
- Updating menu item availability.
- Viewing incoming customer orders.
- Updating order statuses.
- Viewing and handling return requests.
- Monitoring restaurant-related activities.

### 6.3 Delivery Partner Module

The delivery partner module supports delivery-related operations.

Key functionalities include:

- Viewing assigned deliveries.
- Accessing relevant order information.
- Updating delivery progress.
- Managing delivery statuses.
- Marking deliveries as completed.

## 7. Database Design

CampusBite uses Microsoft SQL Server and consists of nine main database tables.

| Table | Description |
|---|---|
| Users | Stores customer, restaurant owner and delivery partner information. |
| Restaurants | Stores restaurant details and information. |
| Menu_Items | Stores food item details, prices and availability. |
| Orders | Maintains customer order information and order statuses. |
| Order_Items | Stores individual food items associated with each order. |
| Reviews | Maintains customer feedback and ratings. |
| Payment_Ledger | Maintains payment-related records. |
| Return_Requests | Stores customer return requests and their statuses. |
| Reminders | Stores order-related reminder information. |

The database uses primary keys and foreign key relationships to maintain connections between related records and support data consistency.

## 8. Implementation and Development

The development of CampusBite was carried out in multiple phases.

**Phase 1 – Frontend Development**

The user interface was developed using React.js and CSS. Separate dashboards and functionalities were created for customers, restaurant owners and delivery partners.

**Phase 2 – Database Development**

The CampusBite database was created using Microsoft SQL Server. Nine relational tables were designed to store the application's information.

**Phase 3 – Backend Development**

FastAPI was used to develop backend endpoints for user authentication, restaurant management, menu operations, orders, reviews and return requests.

SQLAlchemy models and Pydantic schemas were used for database interaction and request validation.

**Phase 4 – Frontend and Backend Integration**

Axios was used to connect the React frontend with the FastAPI backend. API requests were implemented to retrieve and update application data.

**Phase 5 – Containerization**

Docker and Docker Compose were used to package and run the frontend and backend services.

